STEP 2 - client IP resolver (new apps/backend/src/common/http/client-ip.ts)
Exports:
- `type ClientIpScope = 'loopback' | 'private' | 'public' | 'unknown'`.
- `normalizeIp(raw?: string|null): string|null`: trim, strip the `::ffff:` prefix of IPv4-mapped addresses, and return null unless `net.isIP()` accepts it.
- `resolveClientIp(req, trustedHeader?: string): { ip: string|null; source: 'header'|'socket' }`. If trustedHeader is set AND `req.socket.remoteAddress` is loopback (127.0.0.0/8, ::1, ::ffff:127.x), read `req.headers[trustedHeader]`, take the first comma-separated token and normalize it. If valid, return {source:'header'}. Otherwise return { ip: normalizeIp(req.ip), source:'socket' }.
- `clientIpMiddleware(trustedHeader?: string)`: an express middleware that sets `(req as any).clientIp` and `clientIpSource` once per request.
- `clientIpOf(req): string|null`: returns req.clientIp ?? normalizeIp(req.ip).
- `ipScope(ip): ClientIpScope`: uses `net.BlockList`. Loopback is 127/8 and ::1. Private is 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 100.64/10 (CGNAT, typical proxy/edge peers), 169.254/16, fc00::/7 and fe80::/10. Anything else is public; null is unknown.
- `throttleBucket(ip)`: returns an IPv4 address as-is. For IPv6, expand '::' and return the /64 prefix (`v6:xxxx:xxxx:xxxx:xxxx::/64`) so a client cannot rotate addresses within its /64. null returns 'unknown'.
Spec client-ip.spec.ts covers:
- header ignored when trustedHeader is unset;
- header ignored when the peer is not loopback (e.g. 10.0.0.5);
- header used when the peer is 127.0.0.1 or ::ffff:127.0.0.1;
- invalid header value falls back to socket;
- 'a, b' takes the first token;
- ::ffff:1.2.3.4 normalizes to 1.2.3.4;
- scope for 10.1.2.3, 100.64.0.1, 127.0.0.1, 8.8.8.8 and 2a02:2f0e::1;
- two IPv6 addresses in the same /64 give the same bucket.

